      "abstract": "<jats:p>Recognizing procedural activities-such as cooking recipes, furniture assembly, or laboratory protocols-from video requires understanding the temporal ordering and composition of fine-grained action steps. While supervised methods achieve strong results, they require extensive per-step annotations that are impractical to collect at scale for diverse procedural domains. We propose ProtoStep, a few-shot procedural activity recognition framework that learns generalizable temporal prototypes from limited labeled examples. Our approach consists of three components: (1) a Step Prototype Network that constructs prototype representations for each procedural step from few support videos, (2) a Temporal Prototype Alignment mechanism that matches query video sequences to step prototypes through differentiable dynamic time warping, and (3) a Cross-Procedure Transfer module that leverages shared sub-steps across different procedures to improve few-shot generalization. We evaluate ProtoStep on three benchmarks-COIN, CrossTask, and a newly curated ProceduralFew dataset-and show that it outperforms existing few-shot action recognition methods by 4.7% on 5-shot recognition, while being the first method specifically designed for procedural activities in the few-shot setting.</jats:p>",
