      "abstract": "<jats:p>Online temporal action segmentation (TAS) labels each time step without future inputs, yet standard evaluation assumes fresh visual features always arrive. We evaluate feature non-arrival using a strictly causal paired protocol that controls missing fraction and burst duration with Bernoulli and two-state Markov processes. Six causal architectures are tested on GTEA, 50Salads, and Breakfast using last-observation hold and a paired zero-fill control. Of 270 non-clean conditions, 102 met a descriptive metric-inversion rule, but only three survived paired bootstrap and multiplicity-corrected sign-flip tests. In the largest supported case, Accuracy decreased by 0.54 points while F1@50 increased by 5.84 points and transition delay rose by 0.25 steps. At 50% missingness and expected burst length 32, mean Accuracy fell by 6.69 points and delay increased by 8.52 steps. Favorable segmental scores can therefore conceal slower causal reaction. Online TAS evaluation should jointly report frame, segment, and transition-latency metrics under burst-shaped outages.p=0.5</jats:p>",
      "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7412282",
